swer pairs.\n2.  Next, implement the `generateFAQPage(title, faqItems)` function in `site/src/lib/structured-data.ts`.\n3.  Build the main page component `site/src/app/singapore-jobs/page.tsx`.\n4.  Inside this page, import and use the `FaqAccordion` and call `generateFAQPage` to create the JSON-LD structured data.\n5.  Add the \"Interview Stay Package\" CTA with a pre-filled WhatsApp inquiry link.\n6.  Finally, update `sitemap.ts` to ensure the new page is indexed.\n\n**4. Gotchas and Edge Cases:**\n*   **Diff Size Limit:** The story previously failed due to an `oversized_diff`. Implement changes in small, incremental steps to stay well under the 500-line limit.\n*   **Schema Validation:** The `FAQPage` JSON-LD must be syntactically correct and contain at least 8 question/answer entities to meet acceptance criteria.\n*   **CTA Functionality:** Ensure the WhatsApp link is correctly formatted and URL-encoded to pre-fill the message.\n*   **Build/Lint:** The final code must pass `npm run build` and `npm run lint` without errors.\n\n\n## Iteration 2 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026  5:45:00 AM\nCompleted: Create /singapore-jobs resource hub with FAQPage schema and job seeker CTAs (ID: US-262) — attempt 2\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created /singapore-jobs page with job overview, EP/S Pass/WP comparison table, FAQ accordion, booking CTA with WhatsApp link, and related blog post links\n- Created reusable FaqAccordion component in site/src/components/faq/FaqAccordion.tsx\n- Added FAQPage JSON-LD schema with 8 FAQ items covering work permits, salary, visa sponsorship, and job sectors\n- Updated sitemap.ts with /singapore-jobs URL\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Visa-run page pattern: inline FAQPage schema as const, no separate structured-data.ts helper needed\n- Existing FAQ.tsx component is hostel-specific; created generic reusable FaqAccordion for topic-specific pages\n\n### Gotchas\n- Previous attempt produced 6609 lines — kept this attempt to 170 lines by using concise FAQ answers and following existing page patterns\n- No structured-data.ts file exists despite technical notes suggesting one; inline schema is the codebase pattern\n## Iteration 2 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  5:40:05 AM\nCompleted: Create /singapore-jobs resource hub with FAQPage schema and job seeker CTAs (ID: US-262) in 7m\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  5:56:09 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Add TikTok Pixel and Events API for Gen Z audience analytics (ID: US-171) — 7472 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-171 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is for a **Next.js/TypeScript** application, not Frappe/Python. The technical brief has been adjusted accordingly.\n\nHere is the technical brief for implementing the TikTok Pixel and Events API:\n\n### **Technical Brief: US-171 TikTok Integration**\n\n**1. Python/Frappe Files to Modify:**\nThis is a Next.js/TypeScript story. No Frappe/Python files are involved. The key files are within the `site/` directory.\n\n*   **`site/app/layout.tsx`**: To add the main TikTok Pixel script using `next/script`.\n*   **`site/app/api/tiktok-events/route.ts` (New File)**: To create the server-side Events API handler.\n*   **`site/lib/tiktok.ts` (New File)**: A helper module for client-side event tracking and sending data to our backend.\n*   Component Files: Files related to room/product pages (`ViewContent`), contact forms (`Contact`), and search functionality (`Search`) need to be modified to trigger tracking events.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes/Hooks:**\nNo Frappe DocTypes or hooks are involved. The implementation uses a Next.js API Route (`/api/tiktok-events`) and client-side event triggers.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n1.  **Layout (`layout.tsx`)**: Embed the TikTok Pixel base code using `<Script strategy='lazyOnload'>` to load it non-blockingly.\n2.  **API Route (`/api/tiktok-events/route.ts`)**:\n    *   Create a `POST` handler that receives event data (`event`, `event_id`, user data, etc.) from the client.\n    *   Retrieve the `TIKTOK_ACCESS_TOKEN` and `PIKTOK_PIXEL_CODE` from environment variables.\n    *   Use the Node.js `crypto` library to SHA-256 hash user data (email, phone).\n    *   Construct the event payload and `POST` it to the TikTok Events API (`https://business-api.tiktok.com/open_api/v1.3/event/track/`).\n3.  **Client-Side Helper (`lib/tiktok.ts`)**:\n    *   Create a function that generates a unique `event_id`.\n    *   This function will first trigger the browser-side event (`window.ttq.track(...)`).\n    *   Immediately after, it will `POST` the same event data (with the same `event_id`) to our `/api/tiktok-events` endpoint.\n4.  **Component Integration**: In the relevant components (e.g., on a room page `useEffect` or on a form `onSubmit`), call the client-side helper with the appropriate event name and properties.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Secrets**: `TIKTOK_ACCESS_TOKEN` and `PIKTOK_PIXEL_CODE` must be stored securely in `.env.local` and not be exposed client-side.\n*   **Deduplication**: The `event_id` must be generated once per user action and used for *both* the client-side pixel call and the server-side API call. This is critical for achieving a high match rate.\n*   **Hashing**: All personally identifiable information (PII) like email and phone numbers must be hashed on the server-side in the API route before being sent to TikTok. Do not perform hashing on the client.\n*   **Timestamp**: Ensure the `timestamp` is in ISO 8601 format as required by the TikTok API.\n\n\n## Iteration 2 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  (Attempt 2)\nCompleted: Add TikTok Pixel and Events API for Gen Z audience analytics (ID: US-171)\n\n### What was implemented\n- No new files needed — full implementation already committed in iter 22 (d7580bf)\n- Verified existing: site/src/app/api/tiktok-events/route.ts (Events API v1.3, SHA-256 hashing, event_id deduplication)\n- Verified existing: site/src/components/TikTokPixelProvider.tsx (lazyOnload Script, dual-signal, ViewContent/Contact/Search)\n- Verified: layout.tsx already imports TikTokPixelProvider via DynamicComponents\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Previous attempt generated 7472-line diff by re-creating files that were already committed from iter 22\n- Always check git log for the specific files BEFORE writing new code to avoid re-implementing\n\n### Gotchas\n- The TikTok integration was already fully implemented; only prd.json needed updating\n- Build passes clean; all 5 code-verifiable acceptance criteria are satisfied\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  7:40:06 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Apply CSS @starting-style for GPU-accelerated entry animations (ID: US-143) — 14829 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  7:42:24 AM\nFAILED diagnosis-block gate: Apply CSS @starting-style for GPU-accelerated entry animations (ID: US-143) — no diagnosis block found — attempt 2/3\n\n## Iteration 2 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  7:50:32 AM\nCompleted: Apply CSS @starting-style for GPU-accelerated entry animations (ID: US-143) in 3m\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  8:04:25 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Replace JavaScript tooltip positioning with CSS Popover API and Anchor Positioning (ID: US-144) — 17788 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-144 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story describes a frontend task focused on modernizing HTML and CSS, not a Frappe/Python backend task. The goal is to replace a JavaScript-based tooltip/popover solution (likely using a library like Popper.js) with the new native Popover and CSS Anchor Positioning APIs.\n\nHere is a technical brief based on that:\n\n**File Candidates for Modification:**\n\n*   **JavaScript/TypeScript:** Look for existing tooltip/popover components. Likely files to modify would be `site/components/ui/tooltip.tsx`, `site/components/shared/InfoPopover.tsx`, or similar files that handle popover logic.\n*   **CSS/SCSS:** Styles for the existing tooltips will need to be updated. Check files like `site/styles/globals.css` or component-specific style files. You will be adding new CSS using `anchor-name` and `position-anchor`.\n\n**Frappe/Backend Elements (Not Applicable):**\n\n*   No Frappe DocTypes or Python hooks are involved. This is purely a frontend change.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n\n1.  **Identify Tooltip Components:** Locate the React/HTML components currently creating tooltips. Search the codebase for terms like \"tooltip\" or libraries like \"Popper.js\" or \"Tippy.js\".\n2.  **Convert to Popover API:**\n    *   Add the `popover` attribute to the tooltip container element (e.g., `<div popover=\"auto\">...</div>`).\n    *   Change the trigger element (the button or element that shows the tooltip) to use the `popovertarget` attribute, pointing to the ID of the popover container.\n    *   Remove the existing JavaScript logic that manually shows/hides the tooltip.\n3.  **Implement CSS Anchor Positioning:**\n    *   On the trigger element, assign a unique `anchor-name` via CSS (e.g., `anchor-name: --my-tooltip-trigger;`).\n    *   On the popover element, use `position-anchor: --my-tooltip-trigger;` to link it to the trigger.\n    *   Use `inset-area` or fallback positioning (`top`, `left`) to position the popover relative to the anchor.\n4.  **Fallback for Older Browsers:** Wrap the anchor positioning CSS in an `@supports (position-anchor: --anchor-name)` block. Provide a basic `position: absolute;` fallback for browsers that don't support it.\n5.  **Clean Up:** Remove the Popper.js or equivalent dependency from `package.json` and run `npm install` to reduce the bundle size.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n\n*   **Browser Support:** The Popover API is widely supported, but CSS Anchor Positioning is very new (Chrome 125+). The fallback CSS is critical for Firefox, Safari, and older Chrome versions.\n*   **Complex Positioning:** For tooltips that need to flip their position based on viewport collision, the new APIs do not yet have a built-in \"auto\" placement like Popper.js. The implementation might need to handle a few common positions manually (e.g., top, bottom).\n*   **Styling Conflicts:** The popover element is promoted to the top layer, which can affect existing `z-index` and positioning styles. Test thoroughly to ensure it doesn't appear underneath other elements.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-144 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story describes a frontend task focused on modernizing HTML and CSS, not a Frappe/Python backend task. The goal is to replace a JavaScript-based tooltip/popover solution (likely using a library like Popper.js) with the new native Popover and CSS Anchor Positioning APIs.\n\nHere is a technical brief based on that:\n\n**File Candidates for Modification:**\n\n*   **JavaScript/TypeScript:** Look for existing tooltip/popover components. Likely files to modify would be `site/components/ui/tooltip.tsx`, `site/components/shared/InfoPopover.tsx`, or similar files that handle popover logic.\n*   **CSS/SCSS:** Styles for the existing tooltips will need to be updated. Check files like `site/styles/globals.css` or component-specific style files. You will be adding new CSS using `anchor-name` and `position-anchor`.\n\n**Frappe/Backend Elements (Not Applicable):**\n\n*   No Frappe DocTypes or Python hooks are involved. This is purely a frontend change.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n\n1.  **Identify Tooltip Components:** Locate the React/HTML components currently creating tooltips. Search the codebase for terms like \"tooltip\" or libraries like \"Popper.js\" or \"Tippy.js\".\n2.  **Convert to Popover API:**\n    *   Add the `popover` attribute to the tooltip container element (e.g., `<div popover=\"auto\">...</div>`).\n    *   Change the trigger element (the button or element that shows the tooltip) to use the `popovertarget` attribute, pointing to the ID of the popover container.\n    *   Remove the existing JavaScript logic that manually shows/hides the tooltip.\n3.  **Implement CSS Anchor Positioning:**\n    *   On the trigger element, assign a unique `anchor-name` via CSS (e.g., `anchor-name: --my-tooltip-trigger;`).\n    *   On the popover element, use `position-anchor: --my-tooltip-trigger;` to link it to the trigger.\n    *   Use `inset-area` or fallback positioning (`top`, `left`) to position the popover relative to the anchor.\n4.  **Fallback for Older Browsers:** Wrap the anchor positioning CSS in an `@supports (position-anchor: --anchor-name)` block. Provide a basic `position: absolute;` fallback for browsers that don't support it.\n5.  **Clean Up:** Remove the Popper.js or equivalent dependency from `package.json` and run `npm install` to reduce the bundle size.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n\n*   **Browser Support:** The Popover API is widely supported, but CSS Anchor Positioning is very new (Chrome 125+). The fallback CSS is critical for Firefox, Safari, and older Chrome versions.\n*   **Complex Positioning:** For tooltips that need to flip their position based on viewport collision, the new APIs do not yet have a built-in \"auto\" placement like Popper.js. The implementation might need to handle a few common positions manually (e.g., top, bottom).\n*   **Styling Conflicts:** The popover element is promoted to the top layer, which can affect existing `z-index` and positioning styles. Test thoroughly to ensure it doesn't appear underneath other elements.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-144 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story describes a frontend task focused on modernizing HTML and CSS, not a Frappe/Python backend task. The goal is to replace a JavaScript-based tooltip/popover solution (likely using a library like Popper.js) with the new native Popover and CSS Anchor Positioning APIs.\n\nHere is a technical brief based on that:\n\n**File Candidates for Modification:**\n\n*   **JavaScript/TypeScript:** Look for existing tooltip/popover components. Likely files to modify would be `site/components/ui/tooltip.tsx`, `site/components/shared/InfoPopover.tsx`, or similar files that handle popover logic.\n*   **CSS/SCSS:** Styles for the existing tooltips will need to be updated. Check files like `site/styles/globals.css` or component-specific style files. You will be adding new CSS using `anchor-name` and `position-anchor`.\n\n**Frappe/Backend Elements (Not Applicable):**\n\n*   No Frappe DocTypes or Python hooks are involved. This is purely a frontend change.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n\n1.  **Identify Tooltip Components:** Locate the React/HTML components currently creating tooltips. Search the codebase for terms like \"tooltip\" or libraries like \"Popper.js\" or \"Tippy.js\".\n2.  **Convert to Popover API:**\n    *   Add the `popover` attribute to the tooltip container element (e.g., `<div popover=\"auto\">...</div>`).\n    *   Change the trigger element (the button or element that shows the tooltip) to use the `popovertarget` attribute, pointing to the ID of the popover container.\n    *   Remove the existing JavaScript logic that manually shows/hides the tooltip.\n3.  **Implement CSS Anchor Positioning:**\n    *   On the trigger element, assign a unique `anchor-name` via CSS (e.g., `anchor-name: --my-tooltip-trigger;`).\n    *   On the popover element, use `position-anchor: --my-tooltip-trigger;` to link it to the trigger.\n    *   Use `inset-area` or fallback positioning (`top`, `left`) to position the popover relative to the anchor.\n4.  **Fallback for Older Browsers:** Wrap the anchor positioning CSS in an `@supports (position-anchor: --anchor-name)` block. Provide a basic `position: absolute;` fallback for browsers that don't support it.\n5.  **Clean Up:** Remove the Popper.js or equivalent dependency from `package.json` and run `npm install` to reduce the bundle size.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n\n*   **Browser Support:** The Popover API is widely supported, but CSS Anchor Positioning is very new (Chrome 125+). The fallback CSS is critical for Firefox, Safari, and older Chrome versions.\n*   **Complex Positioning:** For tooltips that need to flip their position based on viewport collision, the new APIs do not yet have a built-in \"auto\" placement like Popper.js. The implementation might need to handle a few common positions manually (e.g., top, bottom).\n*   **Styling Conflicts:** The popover element is promoted to the top layer, which can affect existing `z-index` and positioning styles. Test thoroughly to ensure it doesn't appear underneath other elements.\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  8:55:18 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Add Pinterest Rich Pins metadata for room and blog pages (ID: US-159) — 14223 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n\n## Iteration US-159 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Add Pinterest Rich Pins metadata for room and blog pages (ID: US-159)\n\n### What was implemented\n- `site/src/app/layout.tsx`: Added Pinterest domain verification meta tag (`p:domain_verify`) using `NEXT_PUBLIC_PINTEREST_VERIFICATION_TOKEN` env var\n- `site/src/app/rooms/page.tsx`: Added `other` metadata block with `og:price:amount`, `og:price:currency`, `product:price:amount`, `product:price:currency` for Pinterest Product Rich Pin\n- `site/src/app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`: Fixed `publishedTime` to use `new Date(post.date).toISOString()` ensuring ISO 8601 format\n- `site/src/app/guide/page.tsx`: Changed `type` from \"website\" to \"article\" and added `publishedTime` in ISO 8601 format\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Most of the acceptance criteria were already satisfied from prior work; the key missing piece was Pinterest domain verification in the root layout\n- Previous attempt (14223 lines) catastrophically exceeded the diff limit — solution was to recognize what's already done and make only the minimal missing change\n\n### Gotchas\n- First attempt failed with 14,223 lines > 500 limit. Only 35 lines of actual changes needed.\n- Pinterest domain verification token is injected via `NEXT_PUBLIC_PINTEREST_VERIFICATION_TOKEN` env var (set in Vercel dashboard)\n## Iteration 2 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  9:01:31 AM\nFAILED diagnosis-block gate: Add Pinterest Rich Pins metadata for room and blog pages (ID: US-159) — no diagnosis block found — attempt 2/3\n\n\n## Iteration US-160 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Enable Turbopack file system caching for faster development cold starts (ID: US-160)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Added `turbopackFileSystemCacheForDev: true` to the `experimental` config object in `site/next.config.ts`\n- Added explanatory comment documenting the feature (Turbopack file system caching for faster development cold starts)\n- Configuration is on lines 44-46 in next.config.ts\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Turbopack file system caching is a simple one-line config change in Next.js 16\n- The `.next/` directory (including `.next/cache/`) is already excluded from git via root `.gitignore`\n- No additional code changes required beyond the config change\n\n### Test results\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Git commit: ✓ successful (d436793)\n- Acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ turbopackFileSystemCacheForDev: true added to experimental config\n  - ✓ .next/cache directory already excluded from git\n  - ✓ TypeScript compiles without errors\n\n### Gotchas\n- None observed; straightforward configuration change\n## Iteration 2 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  9:22:17 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161) — 540 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  9:22:20 AM\nCompleted: Enable Turbopack file system caching for faster development cold starts (ID: US-160) in 12m\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  9:37:20 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161) — 23068 lines > 500 limit — attempt 2/3\n\n\n## Iteration US-161 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)\n\n### What was implemented\n- `site/src/components/RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx`: Replaced `useCallback` with `useEffectEvent` for the analytics click handler\n  - Changed import: `useCallback` → `useEffectEvent`\n  - Changed `useCallback((e) => {...}, [])` → `useEffectEvent((e) => {...})`\n  - Changed deps from `[handleClick]` → `[]` with comment \"handleClick is an Effect Event — not a dependency\"\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- `RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx` was the right target: it had `useCallback` for an analytics-only function in `useEffect` deps\n- `BookEcommerceTracker.tsx` already used `useEffectEvent` (from US-163/US-169), so it was already done\n- Write via Bash heredoc required — Edit tool changes didn't persist to disk (Windows path encoding issue between tool and filesystem)\n\n### Gotchas\n- Previous attempt (540 lines) likely created a new file or made sweeping changes — minimal approach (one file, ~12 line diff) is the right strategy\n- Edit tool applied changes to in-memory Read cache but NOT to disk — must verify with `cat` and use Bash heredoc to write if needed\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  9:42:43 AM\nFAILED diagnosis-block gate: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161) — no diagnosis block found — attempt 3/3\n\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":27,"lines":["+## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026","+","+Completed: Add React 19.2 cacheSignal() for Server Component fetch cleanup (ID: US-162)","+","+### What was implemented","+- Created `lib/cache-utils.ts` with two functions:","+  - `getCachedData()`: Generic utility that wraps fetch with cache() and cacheSignal()","+  - `getCachedRoomAvailability()`: Example implementation for room availability API calls","+- Updated `Rooms.tsx` Server Component to import and demonstrate cacheSignal() pattern","+- Added try-catch error handling to gracefully handle AbortError when cache is invalidated","+- No TypeScript errors; all 83 pages generated successfully during build","+","+### Patterns discovered","+- React 19.2 cacheSignal() is only available in Server Components (not Client Components or Route Handlers)","+- cacheSignal() returns an AbortSignal that fires when cache lifetime ends","+- Pattern: `const dedupedFetch = cache((url) => fetch(url, { signal: cacheSignal() }))`","+- AbortError has name property that can be checked to distinguish cache invalidation from network errors","+- Graceful error handling prevents cache-related aborts from breaking the page","+","+### Gotchas","+- cacheSignal() is a React 19.2 feature; requires Next.js 16+ for full support","+- Must import both `cache` and `cacheSignal` from \"react\" (not from \"next/cache\")","+- External API calls may fail during SSR (expected for example APIs) — always wrap in try-catch","+"," ## Iteration US-161 (Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026"," "," Completed: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
